<h2>The prosecution and its trajectory</h2>
<p>The FIR, registered at P.S. Hari Nagar in 2021, alleged that the petitioner touched his cousin inappropriately in March 2021, apologised when she protested, and later harassed and threatened her including over WhatsApp. The chargesheet added Section 354D IPC and Section 12 POCSO, and the trial was pending before the ASJ (POCSO), Tis Hazari Courts. The chargesheet itself recorded that the two families were enmeshed in cross-complaints and a money dispute, and the FIR had come six months after the alleged incident. In February 2026 the families executed a memorandum of understanding; the complainant, studying in Canada, authorised her mother by special power of attorney, and then appeared before the High Court in person to confirm that the settlement was voluntary, the families reconciled, and that she had stopped appearing before the trial court and stood dropped as a witness.</p>

<h2>The balancing the Court performed</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>The rule</strong><p>POCSO offences are serious; a child\'s protection is a societal interest that private parties cannot ordinarily bargain away. Settlement alone never suffices.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The discretion</strong><p>Gian Singh leaves quashing to the facts of each case, and Narinder Singh permits the court to ask whether conviction has become a remote and bleak possibility.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>The facts</strong><p>A single non-penetrative allegation near the age of majority, a delayed FIR amid family disputes, an adult complainant who has voluntarily settled, and a trial deprived of its only material witness.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Futility as the touchstone</h2>
<p>The operative reasoning is captured in the Court\'s own words: continuing the proceedings "would be futile and would not serve the ends of justice." That is not a dilution of POCSO — the judgment is explicit that the statute\'s gravity ordinarily defeats compromise-based quashing. It is instead a recognition that criminal trials are instruments, not rituals: where the sole material witness is an adult who has reconciled, relocated and withdrawn, the trial can produce only an acquittal after years of process, burdening the complainant, the accused and the court alike. Earlier Delhi High Court orders and decisions of other High Courts quashing comparable prosecutions were relied upon as part of this line of authority.</p>

<h2>Reading the decision responsibly</h2>
<p>Three cautions belong alongside the result. First, the proximity of the complainant to majority mattered — the Court expressly noted she was roughly two months short of eighteen; the calculus in cases involving young children is entirely different. Second, the nature of the allegation mattered — a single attempted touch followed by an apology is at the lowest end of the POCSO spectrum; penetrative and aggravated offences are quashed on settlement only in the rarest circumstances, if at all. Third, the complainant\'s personal, verified, adult confirmation of voluntariness was the procedural safeguard on which everything else rested. Settlements signed by parents or attorneys without the complainant\'s own confirmed will do not reach this threshold.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>Petitions of this kind in Delhi are now filed under Section 528 of the BNSS, which continues the inherent jurisdiction formerly found in Section 482 CrPC. For incidents after 1 July 2024, the IPC provisions involved here correspond to Sections 74, 78 and 351 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023; the POCSO Act continues unchanged as the special law.</p>
</div>
